Document Understanding: Dev cross-tenant Event Hub implementation runbook

Prepared 8 September 2026 from the supplied publisher, Bicep, Copilot and Platform Engineering screenshots, the available previous-conversation summary, and Microsoft documentation. This is an implementation brief for the clean main-branch clone in your VDI. The actual repository and live Azure resources were not available here, so the examples are integration templates, not an applied or deployed patch. Screenshot line numbers are navigation hints only.

1. The decision that must come before another ID change

The supplied publisher.py is SAS-only: both producer builders call from_connection_string. App registration IDs and an Azure role assignment do not change that code path.

Microsoft’s documentation currently conflicts on system-assigned managed identity (SAMI) as an app’s federated credential. The dedicated managed-identity federation article explicitly says user-assigned only. [1] A newer Microsoft.Identity.Web article, updated 29 April 2026, describes both system-assigned and user-assigned identities. [21] The latter is a .NET guide; it does not by itself verify this Python cross-tenant deployment.

Therefore, do not claim that SAMI is conclusively unsupported everywhere, or that this exact SAMI deployment is guaranteed to work. Preserve your requested system-assigned configuration. First run the token-only Dev probe in section 9 using ManagedIdentityCredential() without a client ID, the real Function principal in the app’s FIC, and the UK resource tenant for the app token. This SDK constructor selects the host’s system identity. [22] Verify the existing trust and the result with Platform before treating the migration as supported and complete.

If the trust is correct but the exchange is rejected because of identity/issuer support, retain the exact error and take it with the conflicting documentation to Platform/Microsoft. The consistently documented alternative is a user-assigned managed identity (UAMI), which can coexist with SAMI [7], but changing the approved identity model is not a code-only fix and is not assumed in this runbook. Do not silently add an identity or introduce secrets.

The missing trust/identity evidence is not proof of the exact cause of the earlier failed deployment. That needs the first failing invocation/token/send evidence. App IDs and RBAC alone still do not supply a usable runtime credential.

